## A B S T R A C T
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derived powder catalysts are prone to agglomerate and difficult to recycle in water, thus resulting in their low utilization and secondary pollution in water treatment. Herein, a composite aerogel $(\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA})$ loaded with bimetallic MOF-derived carbon nanotubes on cellulose aerogel was developed for activating peroxymonosulfate (PMS) to degrade tetracycline (TC). The $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA/PMs}$ system exhibits an excellent TC removal rate ( $97.1\%$ TC removal within $20\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ . The outstanding performance of the composite catalyst is closely related to the synergistic effect of bimetallic cat­ alytic sites, graphitic N structure, and porous network. Interestingly, carbon nanotubes and cellulose in the composite catalyst form a semi-coated porous structure, which can effectively enhance the adhesion of carbon nanotubes and expose abundant active sites while ensuring mass transfer. This study provides a strategy for synthesizing novel composite aerogel with an excellent structure and physicochemical properties for water treatment.
## 1. Introduction
The abuse of antibiotics leads to the accumulation of a large amount of antibiotics, such as tetracycline (TC), in water, which adversely af­ fects the environment and human health (Halling-Sørensen et al., 2002; Wang & Zhuan, 2020; Xu et al., 2021). Thus, it is an urgent need to develop an efficient method for removing antibiotics from natural water. Recently, peroxymonosulfate (PMS)-based advanced oxidation pro­ cesses (AOPs) have become one of the most promising methods in the field of water treatment due to the advantages of strong oxidizing ability, ultra-fast reaction rate, and ideal treatment effect (Fan et al., 2021; Li, Li, et al., 2021; Peng et al., 2022). Moreover, compared with the traditional Fenton oxidation method, PMS-based AOPs have a higher redox potential, longer radical half-life, and wider pH usage range (Giannakis et al., 2021; Kohantorabi et al., 2021; Wang & Wang, 2018). However, the severe reaction conditions of PMS-based AOPs and irre­ versible degradation of catalysts' performance greatly limited their development. The design and synthesis of novel catalysts are the key to the efficient activation of PMS. Therefore, the elaboration of new catalysts with high catalytic activity and good stability is crucial for the development and implementation of AOPs.
The importance of transition metal (such as Co, Fe, Cu, etc.) catalysts in the AOP process has been widely reported (Li, Dong, et al., 2021; Li, Zhu, et al., 2022; Lyu et al., 2019). Multivalent transition metals can enable efficient regulation of redox processes and catalyst electronic structures, while Co and Fe are widely used in wastewater treatment due to their cost-effectiveness and abundant supply (Hou et al., 2020; Li, Li, et al., 2022; Zhao et al., 2021). However, the design of catalysts with proper metal valence state in the PMS system is a great challenge. Due to the tuneable structure and easily modifiable properties, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have received extensive attention in the field of energy storage and conversion, gas adsorption and separation, sensing, and catalysis (Gao et al., 2021; Huang, Zhang, et al., 2021; Wang, Kim, et al., 2019; Wu et al., 2021; Xu et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2019). Multimetals constructed MOFs can maintain the original framework struc­ ture, large specific surface area and provide abundant active sites (Huang, Wang, et al., 2021; Xiong et al., 2021). In addition, the syner­ gistic effect between different metal active sites can also optimize the electronic structure of the catalyst to accelerate electron transfer and redox processes, thereby enhancing the catalytic activity. However, poor electrical conductivity and self-decomposition in water of many MOFs structures greatly limit their application. MOF-derived metal/ carbon materials are rapidly developing due to their high electrical conductivity and chemical stability (Han et al., 2022; Hou et al., 2021; Li, Wu, et al., 2022). The carbonization of MOFs endows nano/micro­ structures with diverse morphologies and abundant accessible active sites. Meanwhile, heteroatom doping (mainly N doping) and defect en­ gineering also favour interfacial electron transfer (Liu et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2022). Thus, the construction of bimetallic MOF-derived N-doped carbon materials is a promising approach to design catalysts for AOPs.
Fabrication of MOFs/MOFs-derivatives into stable three-dimensional (3D) structures is desirable for industrial applications (Cao et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2022; Inonu et al., 2018; Kanti Chattopadhyay & Ranjan Singha, 2021; Li, Ye, et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2022). Recently, Cellulose-based materials as carriers have received extensive attention due to their abundant reserves, environmental protection, and renew­ able advantages (Bethke et al., 2018; Hu et al., 2021; Nasrollahzadeh et al., 2021; Rahmanian et al., 2021). Cellulose aerogel (CA) is an environmentally friendly, ultralight 3D interconnected solid network structure, which provides an efficient method to load MOF-derived carbon materials (Abdelhamid & Mathew, 2022; Chen, Zhang, et al., 2021; Jiao et al., 2018; Lei et al., 2019; Ren et al., 2018). As the support material of MOF-derived carbon materials, CA can effectively help to avoid agglomeration of catalytic material and increase their recycla­ bility (Chen, Li, et al., 2021; Lu et al., 2022; Wang, Xu, et al., 2019). Meanwhile, introducing MOF-derived carbon materials during the syn­ thesis of CA can maximize catalyst dispersion and enhance their adhe­ sion, thereby rapidly expanding the application of CAs.
Herein, this work focuses on the construction of CoFe bimetallic MOF-derived nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube/cellulose composite aerogels $(\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA})$ with high catalytic activity, environ­ mentally friendly, and renewable. Due to the capability of heteroge­ neous cobalt-based catalysts to excellent PMS activation for organic degradation, the bimetallic MOF composed of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ was chosen as the precursor. It is hypothesized that proper $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ doping affects the electron valence state of metal sites in the catalyst, thereby improving the redox process. Moreover, the 3D structure of cellulose aerogels leads to good dispersion and high loading of catalysts on the carrier, which is beneficial to the diffusion of pollutants and the improvement of catalytic efficiency. Various characterization and analytical methods were used to explore the structure and properties of the catalysts and composite aerogels. In addition, the degradation mechanism and cycling stability of the composite aerogel were investigated. These findings have positive implications for developing new efficient porous catalytic materials and expanding the application of biomass materials.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Cotton linter was provided by Heze Sanmu Health Materials Co., Ltd. $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), NaOH, PMS, TC, Urea, $N_{i}$ , $N^{\prime}$ -methylenebisacrylamide (MBA) were obtained from Mclean Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathbf{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{x}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , Sodium Dicyana­ mide $\mathrm{(C_{2}N_{3}N a)}$ were provided by Saen Chemical Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd. Methanol (MA) was supplied by Gaojing Fine Chemical Co., Ltd. Tert-butanol (TBA), p-benzoquinone (P-BZE), Hexamethylenetet­ ramine were purchased from Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. HCl was provided by Huadong Pharmaceutical Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Fabrication of Co-MOF and Fe-MOF
The Co-MOF and Fe-MOF were modified as previously reported (Manna et al., 2006). Hexamethylenetetramine $\left(0.14\mathrm{~g},1\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ and PVP $_{\left.0.06\ g\right.}$ , $\mathbf{Mw}=58,000\mathrm{\textperthousand}$ were dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, then sodium dicyandiamide $(0.18\mathrm{\textrm{g}},\mathrm{\textrm{2}\ m m o l})$ ) was dissolved in $5\ \mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to be mixed with the above solution; $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ $\left(0.29~\ g\right.$ , 1 mmol) was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. Then the Co $\mathrm{(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ solution was slowly added dropwise to the ligand solution under magnetic stirring and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ at room temperature to produce a pink precipitate. The pink MOFs were washed by centrifu­ gation with methanol several times (separated by centrifugation at 8000 rpm using a centrifuge) and subsequently dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , resulting in pink solid powder Co-MOF. $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was replaced with $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}{\bf x}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the Co-MOF preparation method, and other synthesis conditions were kept unchanged to prepare the earthy solid powder Fe-MOF.
## 2.3. Fabrication of CoFex-MOF
Based on the preparation method of Co-MOF, the metal salts Co $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were weighed and dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol according to the molar ratios $\mathsf{\bar{C}o}^{2+}{:\mathsf{F e}}^{2+}=4{:}1$ , 1:1, 1:4, where the total amount of metal salts was 1 mmol. The preparation method of ligand solution was the same as that in Co-MOF. Different ratios of metal ion solutions were slowly added dropwise to the ligand solution under magnetic stirring, and the colour of the solution gradu­ ally changed from pink to earthy yellow since the ratio of Fe increased. The mixed solution was stirred for $6\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature. The CoFexMOFs samples were washed by centrifugation with methanol several times (separated by centrifugation at $8000~\mathrm{rpm}$ using a centrifuge) and subsequently dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ to obtain solid powder, where $\mathbf{x}$ is the ratio of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\bf x}=0.8$ , 0.5, 0.2.
## 2.4. Fabrication of CoFex@NCNT
## 2.5. Fabrication of cellulose hydrogel
The cellulose solution was prepared by mixing NaOH/urea/ $\mathrm{{\cdot}G o}$ at a weight ratio of 7:12:81 and refrigerated at $-4^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, $_{3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of cotton fibres was added to $97~\mathrm{g}$ of the above solution and the mixture solution was frozen at $-20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The frozen cellulose solution was stirred vigorously to obtain transparent dispersion. Next, $0.6\ {\ g}$ of MBA was added to the solution and stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The above solution was poured into an orifice plate and was kept for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ to transform the so­ lution into cellulose hydrogel.
## 2.6. Fabrication of CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA
The homogeneous shearing machine was used to shear the hydrogel at high speed until the hydrogel turned into a thick fluid state. $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ was added with the same quality as cellulose, the content was stirred vigorously to make it fully mixed. Finally, $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ composite aerogel was formed by pouring the uniformly mixed solution into the mold and freezing-drying for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.7. Catalytic experimental details and analysis
Exploring the relationship between the structure and properties of MOFs-derived carbon nanotubes and MOFs-derived carbon nanotubes/ cellulose aerogel composites was performed using dynamic degradation experiments. 10 mg of CoFex@NCNT and 20 mg of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ was added to $50\mathrm{mL}$ of the previously prepared TC solution $(40\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}})$ ), respectively. Then, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of PMS was added for degradation. The degradation efficiency was calculated from $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}_{0}.$ , and the degradation rate constant was calculated from $-\mathrm{Ln}(\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}_{0})$ ${\mathrm{\tilde{C}}}_{0}$ : initial TC concentration, C: TC current concentration at the reaction time). The concentration of TC was measured by UV–Vis spectrophotometer (UV, Hitachi U-3900, Japan) at the characteristic wavelength $(375\mathrm{nm})$ ). 0.1 M NaOH and 0.1 M HCl were used to adjust the pH value of the solution. The cycle test repeats the above operations, $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ composite is washed three times with deionized water and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after each cycle.
