## 3. Results and discussions
Based on the structural tunability of MOF materials, the MOFsderived carbon materials with the bimetallic catalytic sites were con­ structed by adjusting the Fe doping ratio, and the effects of different Fe doping amounts on the active sites and degradation performance of the catalysts were investigated. The prepared CoFex@NCNTs were charac­ terized by SEM. Fig. 1a–e shows the CoFex@NCNT carbon composites doped with different ratios of Fe. It can be observed that all carbon composites show a carbon tube structure. Notably, $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ exhibited the most abundant carbon tube structure, and the carbon tube content gradually decreases with a further increase of Fe doping. When Fe becomes the only metal in the precursor MOFs, the carbon tube morphology is almost missing in Fe@NCNT (Fig. 1e), which transforms into a short tube structure or a small sphere structure. The same results can be observed in the TEM images. As shown in Fig. S1, with the in­ crease of Fe doping, the carbon tube structure gradually decreases until the carbon tube almost disappears and is replaced by a large piece of carbon material covered with metal particles. The metal particles as catalytic sites were observed to be uniformly distributed in the carbon material and wrapped by carbon layers in all samples, as shown in Fig. S1f. This structure can effectively prevent the agglomeration of catalytic sites and erosion of metal catalytic active sites in acidic or alkaline environments. The XRD data can be used to investigate the phase structure of these bimetallic MOFs-derived catalysts. As shown in Fig. 1f, it can be clearly observed that all carbon composites show a distinct diffraction peak around $26^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (002) crystal plane of graphitic carbon (PDF#75-1621). This result demonstrates the carbon structure features of CoFex $@{\mathsf{N C N T}}$ . The diffraction peaks located at $44.1^{\circ}$ and $51.4^{\circ}$ correspond to the (111) and (200) planes of Co metal and Fe metal, respectively, which indicate that the catalytic sites wrapped in the carbon layer are monometallic particles (Ren, Duan, et al., 2020; Xue et al., 2020).
The BET specific surface area test was used to investigate the pore structure of the catalytic materials. As shown in Fig. S2a–e, all the curves exhibit typical type IV isotherms, which produced a significant hyster­ esis loop in the relative pressure range of 0.4–1.0, proving the meso­ porous structure of the materials. As shown in Fig. S2f, the comparison of the specific surface areas of the catalysts revealed that $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ had the largest specific surface area of $329.5~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The BET specific surface area of $\mathtt{C o F e0.5@N C N T}$ , $\mathtt{\odot0F e0.2@N C N T}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{NCNT}$ decreased gradually to $241.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $156.6\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $131.7\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively, which also can be attributed to the reduced contents of the carbon nanotubes. The BET results combined with SEM results proved that $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ possessed the most abundant carbon nanotubes, which could provide a larger specific surface area for the catalytic materials, helping to promote the transport of active sub­ stances (M. Li et al., 2015).
The high-resolution spectra of N 1s, Co 2p and Fe 2p of CoFex@NCNT were analysed by XPS to further revealed the valence changes of the active components and the deep catalytic mechanism. Ndoped carbon materials are formed from MOFs precursors during hightemperature carbonization, which is one of the important factors affecting the redox reaction (Zhang et al., 2014). Fig. 2a showed the high-resolution spectra of N 1s in MOFs-derived carbon materials with four states of N, including pyridine N (398.6 eV), pyrrole $\Gamma(400.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ , graphite N (401.6 eV), and oxidized N (404.0 eV) (Miao et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2021; Zhou et al., 2020), due to the high thermal stability of graphitic N at high temperatures, a part of pyridine N and pyrrolic N were converted into graphitic N, resulting in the largest proportion of graphitic N (Chen et al., 2018). In addition, graphite N possesses a higher electronegativity and can attract more electrons. Therefore, the coexistence of graphite N with Co and Fe monometallic particles can accelerate the electron transfer between metal components and PMS, accelerating the redox process (Ding et al., 2020; Sun et al., 2021).
Notably, $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ had the highest graphitic N content compared to other catalysts (Table S1), which was one of the factors that signifi­ cantly increases the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ . The Co $\mathsf{2p}$ high-resolution spectrum (Fig. 2b) shows the presence of three states of Co, namely $\mathsf{C o}^{0}(778.3\mathrm{eV})$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ (781.6 and $796.4\mathrm{eV})$ , and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}(779.8$ and $793.8\mathrm{eV},$ (Chen & Wang, 2021; Li et al., 2020; Qi et al., 2021; Yao et al., 2016). The presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ indicated that part of the Co species is oxidized in the air environment. Notably, $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ exhibited the highest $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ ratio (1.03) containing compared to $\mathrm{CoFe0.5@NCNT}$ (0.99) and $\mathrm{CoFe0.2@NCNT}$ (0.92), indicating a higher ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ content on the surface of Co species in $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ . The high content of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ helps to promote the redox reaction and endow $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ with higher electron conversion efficiency. The Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra of different samples were shown in Fig. 2c. The deconvoluted peaks of the Fe 2p spectra can be classified as $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ (707.8 and $719.9\mathrm{eV})$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ (713.5 and 727.1 eV) and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (710.5 and 722.1 eV) (Wang et al., 2020; Xie et al., 2021), with small peaks at ${\sim}716.0$ and ${\sim}733.0\mathrm{eV}$ as the corresponding satellite peaks. The presence of species ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ en­ sures a reversible redox reaction between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}/{\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in the catalytic process, providing catalytic activity of the catalyst for the activation of PMS. The effect of Fe doping will be explained in the degradation mechanism section.
The TC degradation performance of CoFex@NCNT is shown in Fig. S3a. It can be observed that all catalysts had degradation effects on TC, and $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ showed the most significant degradation effi­ ciency, reaching more than $95\%$ within 7 min. As shown in Fig. S3b, the degradation rate constant of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ of $0.803\mathrm{\min}^{-1}$ was observed from the calculated pseudo first-order kinetic model, which is higher than for other samples. The above results prove that abundant carbon nanotube structure and the synergistic effect of the bimetallic active catalytic sites endow the optimal degradation efficiency of
CoFe0.8@NCNT. CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA composite with the semiencapsulated structure was obtained by combining $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ and regenerated cellulose. The photos of pure cellulose aerogel and CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA composite were shown in Fig. 3b and c, CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA composite exhibited the black colour because of the CoFe0.8@NCNT component, while the pure cellulose aerogel is white.
The composite aerogel was prepared by doping method as shown in Fig. 3a, and the specific synthesis is detailed in experimental section. As shown in Fig. 3d and e, the pure cellulose aerogel exhibited porous structure and the surface of the pore channels appeared smooth, which was produced by the extrusion of regenerated cellulose from ice crystals during freezing. In the composite aerogel (Fig. 3f), the porous skeleton structure is maintained, which effectively promoted the diffusion and adsorption of contaminants. $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ was successfully loaded onto the pore surface and uniformly attached to the pore surface. It is worth noting that the MOF-derived carbon nanotubes form a semiencapsulated structure with the cellulose aerogel. As shown in Fig. ${3}\mathrm{g},$ a portion of the MOF-derived carbon nanotubes attached to the aerogel surface is wrapped in regenerated cellulose, and another portion allows the nanotubes to accumulate into a porous structure. The semiencapsulated structure can effectively enhance the adhesion of carbon nanotubes and prevent their dislodgement, and the structure can still expose a very large number of active sites. The porous structure of aerogel not only plays the role of a support for carbon nanotubes, but also facilitates the transport and enrichment of pollutants. The pore structure of aerogel combined with the unique MOF-derived carbon nanotube can effectively improve the catalytic efficiency.
The XRD method was used to investigate the phase changes of the composites during their formation. As shown in Fig. S5, the XRD pattern of the composite aerogel showed a pure aerogel bulge peak (at about $20^{\circ}\mathrm{:}$ ), a graphitic carbon diffraction peak $26.5^{\circ}$ and characteristic peaks of metal catalytic sites $(44.5^{\circ}$ and $53.0^{\circ}.$ ), demonstrating that $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ was successfully loaded on the cellulose aerogel. Combined with the SEM images of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA}$ the structure of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ did not change during the composite formation process, which proved that the doping method did not affect the morphology and structure of the $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ . The loading amount of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ in the aerogel was calculated from the formula in literature (Zhu et al., 2016) by (TGA (Fig. S4 and Table S2). The $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA}$ possesses a high loading amount of CoFe0.8@NCNT $(53.1~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , which is beneficial to promoting catalytic performance. The porosity of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ increased from 92.7 $\%$ (CA) to $96.7\%$ (Table S3), demonstrating that the composite aerogel prepared using the doping method can still maintain high porosity. Besides, the total pore area and apparent density were increased from $0.709\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $2.556\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and $1.23\mathrm{~g~mL}^{-1}$ to $2.49\mathrm{~g~mL}^{-1}$ , respectively. And the average pore diameter decreased from $57.82\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to $18.44~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . All the results demonstrated that $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ inherited the unique properties of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ .
The degradation performance of different systems for TC was investigated as shown in Fig. 4. The pure cellulose aerogel did not work for the contaminant degradation. The addition of PMS to the degrada­ tion of pure cellulose aerogel showed a slight decrease in the degrada­ tion curve, indicating a small amount of TC was degraded, which was attributed to the reactive radicals produced by partial hydrolysis of PMS (Ding et al., 2020; Ghanbari & Moradi, 2017). The curve of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ alone showed a small decrease, which was caused by the slight adsorption of carbon nanotubes and cellulose aerogel. The degradation reaction occurred rapidly when $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ and PMS were present simultaneously, which proved that the catalyst $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ could effectively activate PMS to produce free radicals for efficient degradation of TC. The degradation process reached equi­ librium within $20\mathrm{min}$ , while the removal rate of TC could reach to 97.1 $\%$ . The catalytic performance of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ is better than those of the latest research results, as tabulated Table S4. The above results show that the $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ composite aerogel can effectively activate PMS to efficiently remove TC during the degradation process, and the $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ material plays the prominent catalytic role in the composite aerogel.
The influencing factors of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ in the degradation process are essential parameters in the practical application of the catalyst. Fig. 5a demonstrated the degradation curves of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ at different $\mathsf{p H}$ values. It was found that the degradation rate of TC decreased with increasing $\mathfrak{p H}$ . At $\mathbf{p}\mathbf{H}=9$ , the removal rate of TC decreased to $92.3~\%$ . In alkaline conditions, $s0_{4}\bullet^{-}$ radicals are more likely to react with $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ to generate ⋅OH radicals, which are then converted into inactive $\cdot_{0}-$ radicals (Yang et al., 2022). Fig. 5b showed the effect of the initial concentration of TC on the degradation efficiency of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ . The degradation effi­ ciency increased since the concentration of TC from 30 to $40\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . However, the degradation efficiency was reduced significantly when the concentration is $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ , and the TC removal rate drops to $90.8~\%$ , indicating that the oxidation capacity of the active species produced by the catalytic reaction reaches saturation. The effect of the amount of
PMS on the catalytic degradation efficiency was shown in Fig. 5c. When the amount of PMS was increased from 0.4 to $0.6\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ , the catalytic degradation efficiency increased significantly. The removal rate of TC increased from $82.8~\%$ to $92.8~\%$ within $10~\mathrm{min}$ . However, the degra­ dation efficiency decreased when the amount of PMS continued to in­ crease to $0.8\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ and $1\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , with the removal rate of TC decreasing to $77.0~\%$ and $78.5~\%$ in $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , respectively, which proved that the excessive amount of PMS inhibited the degradation reaction instead (Ren, Zhu, et al., 2020). Fig. 5d showed the effect of the amount of the catalyst on the degradation performance. The degradation efficiency increased rapidly when the amount of the catalyst increased to $0.6\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ from $0.3\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The corresponding removal rate of TC increased from $86.4~\%$ to $98.7~\%$ , which resulted from the increase in catalytic active sites. It was found that the removal rate of TC decreased from $98.7\%$ to $97.0\%$ when the amount of the catalyst increased from $0.6\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ to $0.9{\ g\$ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ , indicating that the degradation efficiency no longer increases after the saturation of highly reactive oxygen molecules activated by the catalytic site.
Studying the catalytic mechanism is an essential part of efficient catalyst design. To investigate the components that play a decisive role in the catalytic process, radical trapping tests and EPR tests were per­ formed. As shown in Fig. 6a, the catalytic efficiency of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ decreased slightly when TBA (for capturing sul­ fate radicals) and MA (for capturing hydroxyl radicals) were added, and the removal of TC decreased from $98.6~\%$ to $93.2~\%$ and $88.7~\%$ , respectively. However, the removal rate of TC significantly reduced to about $67\%$ when $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{p}}$ -benzoquinone (for capturing superoxide radicals) was added, indicating that superoxide radicals play a major role in the degradation process. Thus, the importance of active radicals in the catalytic degradation process of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ was ranked as: superoxide radical $>$ hydroxyl radical $>$ sulfate radical. The EPR test can clearly detect the reactive groups generated during the catalytic process, including sulfate radicals, hydroxyl radicals and superoxide radicals, as shown in Fig. 6b and c.
The degradation mechanism of TC is divided into two processes. First, the hydrolysis of PMS leads to the formation of $0_{2}\cdot^{-}$ (Eq. S1). Second, a redox reaction occurs between ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ and PMS. ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ can effectively activate PMS to generate •OH (Eqs. S2 and S3). At the same time, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ are oxidized to form ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ . Since the redox potential of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co}^{2+}/\mathbf{Co}^{3+}$ (1.92 V) is between those of $\mathrm{HSO_{5}^{-}/S O_{4}^{-}}$ (2.5–3.1 V) and $\mathrm{HSO}_{5}^{-}/\mathrm{SO}_{5}^{-}$ (1.1 V), the activation of PMS is thermo­ dynamically feasible (Sun et al., 2021). Moreover, PMS can promote $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ cyclic transformation (Eqs. S4 and S5). The redox potentials of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ are $0.77{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $0.44\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. There­ fore, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ can transfer electrons to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and promote ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ regeneration (Eqs. S6 and S7). In addition, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ can also activate PMS to produce $s0_{4}\cdot^{-}$ (Eq. S8), and a redox reaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ promotes the regeneration of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Eq. S9). However, the redox po­ tential of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ transition is lower than that of the $\mathrm{HSO}_{5}^{-}/{\mathrm{SO}}_{5}^{-}$ transition, indicating that there is no effective interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and PMS (Sun et al., 2016). Therefore, the redox cycles of $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\leftrightarrow$ $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}\to{\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}\leftrightarrow{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ are both effective in activating PMS to continuously generate oxidative radicals (ROS) and react with TC to produce $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (Eq. S10). During the catalytic process, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Fe}}^{0}$ can effectively promote electron transfer between catalysts and PMS, and increase the active radical generation rate. The suggested catalytic degradation mechanism scheme is shown in Fig. 7.
The durability of the material is also a critical evaluation index during practical application. Fig. S6a shows the cyclic degradation performance of $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA}.$ . The results showed that the degradation and removal efficiency of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ composite aerogel can still reach about $90\%$ after three cycles of TC degradation, which proves that the $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ composite aerogel has good recyclability. As shown in Fig. S6b, the XRD characteristic peaks of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ after three cycles were the same as that of $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ before recycling, which proved the high stability of the metal catalytic site encapsulated in the carbon nanotubes. How­ ever, the peak at $26.5^{\circ}$ corresponding to the graphitic carbon almost disappears from the XRD pattern of the $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ sample after three cycles. This is attributed to the detachment of part $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT}$ . SEM images (Fig. S6c and d) showed that $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ could maintain its morphology with mesoporous structure and the semi-encapsulated structure after several cycling tests, proving the high stability of the structure.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, a cellulose-based composite aerogel loaded with bimetallic MOF-derived carbon nanotubes $\mathtt{\langle C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A)}$ was developed. It was proved that appropriate Fe doping can effectively promote the generation of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and optimize the structure of graphitic N, thus ensuring good electron transfer ability. Besides, carbon nano­ tubes and regenerated cellulose form a semi-coated porous structure, which can effectively enhance the adhesion of carbon nanotubes and expose more active sites while ensuring the mass transfer path. These results strongly confirmed our hypothesis. Thus, $\mathtt{C o F e0.8@N C N T@C A}$ possessed good catalytic activity, and the degradation rate of TC is 97.1 $\%$ in $20\mathrm{min}$ by using $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA/PMS}$ system. It is discovered that the CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA/PMS system is dominated by the direct electron transfer mechanism and the radical mechanism. Moreover, the $\mathrm{CoFe0.8@NCNT@CA/PMS}$ system possesses strong stability and excel­ lent regenerability. Thus, the study may provide new ideas for designing novel composite aerogels with excellent structure and performance.
